  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Very humid with locally heavy rainfall possible \nthis afternoon\n\nHighly anomalous moisture for this time of year (NAEFS mean \npwat values near climatological maximum percentiles) pooling \naround the periphery of subtropical high over the southern \nPlains and lower MS Valley will interact with a surface cold \nfront moving slowly south/southeastward from the lower Great \nLakes to produce periods of showers and a few locally heavy \nthunderstorms across Pennsylvania later today. 17/00Z HREF \nindicates the potential for spot rain amounts of 2-3 inches. \nIt's very likely that most of central PA picks up some rainfall\nby tonight. However, we do not anticipate an all-day rain/washout.\nCloud trends remain a tough call with early morning convection\nover north central PA, but fcst instability this afternoon across\nsouth central PA will be more than supportive for thunderstorm \ndevelopment. SPC continues to maintain a general (non-severe) \nconvective outlook for today which is likely due to the lack of \ndeep layer shear (displaced farther to the north) and poor mid \nlevel lapse rates (very moist, tall/skinny soundings with warm \ncloud depths greater than 10kft). \n\nAnother taste of Fall is on the way for the end of the week \nwith noticeably cooler and much drier/less humid air poised to \nreturn behind the front (Td crashing into the 40s/50s). Expect a\ncrisp and cool overnight Friday into Saturday morning with some\nupper 30s possible across the northern tier. Dry/fair wx \nconditions are forecast to start the weekend and last through \nthe day on Saturday followed by periods of potentially heavy \nrainfall Saturday night through early next week. Both NAEFS and\nECMWF SATs show at least 90th percentile PWs returning the the\nregion which is supportive for at least a marginal excessive\nrain signal. NBM temps have also cooled/moderated quite a bit \nMon-Wed with most locations struggling to exceed 70F for \ndaytime highs.",
